<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Make a Poster! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Make a Poster!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Contextualize simple school announcements according to local needs. Text: Simplify textbook content for peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can contextualize school announcements using gestures or visuals to highlight key details (e.g., pointing to Friday on a calendar and saying, "The poster event is on Friday." or showing a pair of scissors and saying, "We will make the poster tomorrow."). Can simplify textbook content with basic words and visuals (e.g., pointing to a picture and saying, "This book is about instructions."). Can simplify classroom instructions or textbook content for peers using simple words and visuals (e.g., showing a picture of scissors and paper and saying, "Cut the paper like this." or pointing to a step-by-step poster and saying, "First, cut the paper. Then, glue it on the poster.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows realia: a piece of paper, scissors, and glue. The teacher asks, "What can we make with these?" Students brainstorm ideas like "poster," "card," "mask." Teacher then shows a simple, unfinished poster. "Today, we are going to learn how to explain how to make something, like this poster, to our friends."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a very simple two-step instruction, using gestures and simple words. For example: "First, take the <b>paper</b>. Next, <b>cut</b> it." (Teacher demonstrates with realia). Teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /p/ in "paper" and /s/ in "scissors" as she demonstrates. "Look, /p/aper, /s/cissors."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the first thing I do?" (Students respond: "Take the paper.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I cut first or glue first?" (Students respond: "Cut first.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Following Instructions at School")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple flowchart poster titled "How to Make a Paper Mask." The flowchart uses simple images and short imperative sentences with sequencing words (e.g., "First, draw eyes. Next, cut the mask."). It also includes a short school announcement about an "Art Project Video" due next week. The teacher points out the word "classroom" and divides it into syllables "class-room" with a clap.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to contextualize simple written instructions and announcements, preparing to simplify them for peers using visual aids.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher displays the flowchart and the announcement.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are these instructions about?" (Students answer: "How to make a mask.") "What is the announcement about?" (Students answer: "An art project video.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The first step is to cut the paper. True or False?" (False, it's 'draw'). "The video project is next month. True or False?" (False, it's 'next week').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher asks: "What do we need for the mask?" Students point to images of <b>paper</b>, <b>scissors</b>, <b>pencil</b> on the flowchart. "When is the video due?" Students point to "next week" on the announcement.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Contextualizing and simplifying instructions using plural imperatives ("Let's..."), sequencing words ("first, next"), future tense ("I will...", "I am going to..."), and vocabulary (paper, scissors, glue, pencil, poster, cut, fold, paste, draw). Asking for clarification: "What do I need to do next?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Ordering):</b> Students receive strips of paper with jumbled instructions for making a simple item (e.g., "draw a face," "cut the eyes," "glue it on a stick"). In pairs, they arrange them in the correct order, using "First," "Next," etc. They then write the ordered steps on a worksheet. E.g., "First, draw a face. Next, cut the eyes."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Instructional Role-play):</b> Teacher gives each pair a different simple object to "make" (e.g., a paper flower, a small card, a decoration). One student has simple written instructions (e.g., "Fold paper in half. Draw a flower. Cut the flower."). The other student has only a picture of the final product. Student A gives instructions to Student B, using words like "First," "Next," and gestures. Student B can ask: "What do I need to do next?" Student A simplifies the instruction if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Building:</b> Groups of three. Each group gets three picture cards showing a simple classroom routine (e.g., "clean up books," "put away crayons," "share pencils"). They write down three sentences describing the routine using sequencing words and future tense. E.g., "First, we will clean up the books. Next, we are going to put away the crayons. Then, we will share the pencils." The teacher monitors for correct use of plural imperatives ("Let's clean up!"), sequencing, and future tense.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Following Instructions at School")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Instructional Video Planning and Presentation. Students work in groups of 3-4 to plan and present their "Instructional Video" for the 21st Century Skills Project (demonstrating a classroom routine). They will mediate the routine by explaining it clearly for an audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group chooses a classroom routine (e.g., how to organize the <b>classroom</b> for art, how to prepare for a <b>project</b>, how to clean up after snack time).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They plan the steps for their video using simple sentences and sequencing words. They also decide who will do what ("I will hold the <b>scissors</b>," "We are going to <b>paste</b> the <b>paper</b>"). They will need to explain this routine to their peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups create a simple "flowchart" (like the mask example) on a large <b>paper</b> or <b>cardboard</b> to represent their routine steps using words and drawings. This is their mediation tool to simplify the process.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents their planned routine to the class. One student explains each step, pointing to their flowchart, using simple words and gestures to help others understand. Other group members demonstrate. They use plural imperatives ("Let's arrange the chairs!") and future tense ("First, I will open the paint. Then, we are going to draw a big flower on the poster.") They explicitly use target vocabulary such as <b>paint</b>, <b>crayon</b>, <b>pencil</b>, <b>draw</b>, <b>create</b>, <b>share</b>, <b>decorate</b>.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist during the group presentations, focusing on how well students mediate the instructions for their peers in the instructional video planning. The groups use their flowcharts as a visual aid to mediate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanation:</b> Explains steps clearly using simple words and gestures (Mediation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Includes words like <b>paper</b>, <b>scissors</b>, <b>glue</b>, <b>cut</b>, <b>paste</b>, <b>draw</b>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of grammar:</b> Uses sequencing words (first, next) and plural imperatives ("Let's..."), future tense ("I will...", "We are going to...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works effectively with group members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Visual aids:</b> Uses flowchart effectively to simplify instructions.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used to explain something today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain 'how to make a ______' using 'First...' and 'Next...' (Yes/No/A little bit)."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining our video plan was easy for me?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult? What do I need to practice more to explain things better?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group project provided a good opportunity for students to apply mediation skills and use target language. Most students were able to use sequencing words and imperatives effectively when explaining their routine. Some struggled with consistent use of future tense and simplifying complex steps into very basic English. More guided practice on rephrasing for clarity and using visual cues will be beneficial. The pronunciation practice of /p/ and /s/ was integrated well when describing materials like "paper" and "scissors."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Let's Make a Poster!" theme. The next lesson will begin a new theme, likely building on collaborative skills and instruction following in a new context, perhaps with a focus on a different skill such as listening for specific details in more complex instructions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>